<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it allowed to remind a patient to book a follow-up appointment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — a scheduling reminder framed as administrative coordination is standard healthcare practice. The line to stay clear of is any language implying a guaranteed outcome or medical necessity, which should only come from the practitioner within an actual consultation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a clinic offer a membership or package deal for recurring visits?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, package pricing and membership-style arrangements are common — the framing simply needs to stay administrative rather than describing clinical outcomes or results."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I win back a lapsed patient without a confidentiality risk?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Keep the message generic and warm, send it privately one-to-one, and never reference what they were treated for or why they stopped coming."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should follow-up reminders come from the practitioner or the front desk?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Administrative scheduling reminders are appropriately handled by front desk or coordinator staff. Anything touching on treatment necessity or expected outcomes should come from the practitioner directly, within a consultation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What retention rate is realistic for a UAE aesthetic clinic?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"This varies significantly by treatment type and patient base, so there's no single benchmark, but clinics with a consistent, compliant follow-up system typically see meaningfully higher rebooking rates than clinics with no system at all."}}]}</script>

			
			
